<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Revolution</p><h1 class="enc-title">February Revolution</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1917">1917</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The February Revolution of 1917 was the uprising in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petrograd">Petrograd</a> that forced the abdication of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tsar">Tsar</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicholas-ii">Nicholas II</a>. It ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-romanov">Romanov</a> monarchy and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a>, establishing a provisional government that was overthrown by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolsheviks">Bolsheviks</a> later that year.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/february-revolution.jpg" alt="February Revolution" width="320" height="193" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jones, Stinton · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Manifestaci%C3%B3nAFavorDeLaRep%C3%BAblicaPetrogrado1917--russiainrevolut00jone.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The revolution took place when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> still used the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julian-calendar">Julian calendar</a>, which is why it is called the February Revolution although its events fell in March by the Gregorian calendar. Historians disagree about its main causes. Liberal historians stress the turmoil of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>, Marxists the inevitability of change, and revisionists land disputes after the end of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serfdom">serfdom</a>. Long-term causes included the failure of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Imperial Russia</a> to modernise its structures while keeping loyalty to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/autocracy">autocracy</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-revolution-of-1905">Revolution of 1905</a>.</p><p>By early 1917 the Russian army had suffered about 9.15 million casualties, including some 1.7 million killed, and desertion ran at around 34,000 a month. The authority of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicholas-ii">Nicholas II</a> was further weakened by the assassination of Rasputin on 30 December 1916 and by a rapid turnover of ministers.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Workers at the Putilov Factory went on strike on 18 February Old Style (3 March New Style). On 23 February Old Style (8 March) they were joined by women marking International Women's Day and protesting against food rationing. By the next day nearly 200,000 protesters were in the streets, and by 25 February some 250,000 were on strike and nearly all industrial enterprises in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petrograd">Petrograd</a> had shut down.</p><p>Many soldiers of the garrison, largely draftees of low morale, joined the crowds, and on 27 February Old Style most of it sided with the revolutionaries. Duma members formed a Provisional Committee, and the socialist parties re-established the Petrograd Soviet. The committee took control of the railway <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telegraph">telegraph</a> and forbade trains to travel within 265 kilometres of Petrograd, which made it very hard for loyal troops to reach the capital. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicholas-ii">Nicholas II</a>, stranded on his train at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pskov">Pskov</a>, was forced to abdicate on the advice of his generals. Over 1,300 people were killed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>A Provisional Government, at first under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgy-lvov">Georgy Lvov</a>, replaced the Council of Ministers. Its manifesto promised civil and political rights and a Constituent Assembly but did not address the war or land. It shared authority with the Petrograd Soviet in an arrangement known as dual power; the Soviet had more practical power because it controlled the workers and soldiers.</p><p>After the July Days, in which the government killed hundreds of protesters, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-kerensky">Alexander Kerensky</a> became head of the government. He could not resolve food shortages and mass unemployment and kept <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> in the war. These failures led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/october-revolution">October Revolution</a> by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolsheviks">Bolsheviks</a>, and the collapse of authority contributed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-civil-war">Russian Civil War</a> and the formation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “February Revolution” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After <b>the February Revolution</b> of 1917 the Russian Provisional Government created the Autonomous Governorate of Estonia, uniting the Estonian-speaking districts of the old Estonian and Livonian governorates, and an elected provincial assembly, the Maapäev, met in Tallinn.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-estonia">Republic of Estonia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>It remained the anthem until the <b>February Revolution</b> of 1917.</blockquote><a href="/songs/god-save-the-tsar">God Save the Tsar!</a></li><li><blockquote>In Russia, Peter Lavrov wrote new Russian words to the melody in 1875; this &quot;Worker's Marseillaise&quot; was sung in the Revolution of 1905 and served as the semi-official anthem of the Russian republic after the <b>February Revolution</b> of 1917.</blockquote><a href="/songs/la-marseillaise">La Marseillaise</a></li><li><blockquote>After the <b>February Revolution</b>, Russia's Provisional Government decreed on 12 April 1917 that the Governorate of Estonia would be joined with the Estonian-speaking northern districts of the Governorate of Livonia, uniting the Estonian people in one autonomous province.</blockquote><a href="/state/autonomous-governorate-of-estonia">Autonomous Governorate of Estonia</a></li><li><blockquote>After the <b>February Revolution</b> of 1917 Bashkir nationalists led by Ahmet-Zaki Validi (Zeki Velidi Togan) held congresses in Orenburg and Ufa.</blockquote><a href="/state/bashkurdistan">Bashkurdistan</a></li><li><blockquote>After the <b>February Revolution</b>, Crimean Tatars organised to win self-government.</blockquote><a href="/state/crimean-peoples-republic">Crimean People's Republic</a></li></ul></section>
